# 🔺 CAP Theorem — System Design Concept (Interview Revision Guide)

> **Appears in**: Every HLD interview when discussing database choices, replication, and trade-offs.

---

## 📌 What is CAP Theorem?

CAP says that in a distributed system, **when a network partition happens**, you can only guarantee **two out of three**:

| Property | Meaning | Simple Version |
|----------|---------|----------------|
| **C — Consistency** | Every read gets the **latest committed** value | "All nodes see the same data at the same time" |
| **A — Availability** | Every request gets a **non-error response** (may be stale) | "The system always responds, even if the data might be old" |
| **P — Partition Tolerance** | The system **keeps working** despite network failures between nodes | "Nodes can't talk to each other, but the system doesn't crash" |

---

## ⭐ The Correct Way to Think About It

People often say: *"CAP means you choose 2 out of 3."*

**That's an oversimplification.** The accurate statement is:

> **When a network partition occurs, a distributed system must choose between Consistency and Availability.**

Why can't you sacrifice **P**? Because networks **will** fail. Cables break. Data centers go offline. A system that can't handle partitions isn't distributed — it's a single machine.

```
Normal operation (no partition):
    C + A + P → all three can appear achievable ✅

During a network partition:
    P is forced on you (network broke — you have no choice)
    
    You MUST pick:
            P (forced)
           / \
          C   A
       pick   pick
       one    one
```

