## 🔄 Consistency Models (Beyond CAP)

CAP's "Consistency" is **strong consistency**. But there's a spectrum:

```
Strong ◄─────────────────────────────────────────────► Eventual
Consistency                                           Consistency

"All nodes see the                              "Eventually all nodes
 same data instantly"                            will converge to the
                                                 same value... someday"

   ┌──────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────┐
   │Strong│    │Lineariz- │    │  Causal     │    │ Eventual │
   │      │    │ability   │    │ Consistency │    │          │
   └──────┘    └──────────┘    └─────────────┘    └──────────┘
   Bank         Google          Social media       DNS, CDN
   balance      Spanner         (comments)         caching
```

| Model | Meaning | Example |
|-------|---------|---------|
| **Strong Consistency** | After a write, ALL subsequent reads return the new value | Bank: after transfer, balance is immediately correct everywhere |
| **Eventual Consistency** | Reads may return stale data, but will **eventually** converge | Instagram: your new post might take 2 seconds to appear for followers |
| **Causal Consistency** | If event A caused event B, everyone sees A before B | Chat: if you reply to a message, no one sees the reply before the original |
| **Read-your-own-writes** | The user who wrote the data always sees their own writes | Profile update: YOU see your new name immediately, others may lag |

### Interview Tip: Most Systems Use Eventual Consistency

> 🎤 *"In your Uber design, is the driver's location strongly consistent?"*  
> **Answer**: No — eventual consistency is fine. If the rider sees the driver's position from 2 seconds ago, that's acceptable. Strong consistency for location would be overkill and add unnecessary latency.

