## ❓ Interview Cross-Questions & Answers

> **Q: "Can a system be both consistent and available?"**  
> A: Yes — **when there's no partition**. During normal operation, a well-designed system can provide both. CAP only forces a trade-off **during a network partition**. That's why the accurate statement is: "During a partition, choose C or A."

> **Q: "Is MongoDB CP or AP?"**  
> A: **CP by default.** Writes go to the primary node. If the primary is unreachable (partition), the cluster holds an election for a new primary. During the election (~10-30 seconds), writes are unavailable. It sacrifices availability for consistency. But you CAN configure MongoDB to read from secondaries (at the cost of consistency).

> **Q: "Is Cassandra AP or CP?"**  
> A: **AP by default.** Writes can go to any node. During a partition, both sides accept writes independently. When the partition heals, Cassandra resolves conflicts using **last-write-wins** (timestamp-based). You CAN configure Cassandra for stronger consistency using quorum reads/writes (read from majority of nodes).

> **Q: "What about Google Spanner? It claims to be CA."**  
> A: Spanner achieves **strong consistency + high availability** by using **TrueTime** (atomic clocks + GPS) to keep all nodes synchronized within ~7ms. It's technically CP — during a partition, it chooses consistency. But Google's private network makes partitions so rare that it *appears* CA in practice. It's an engineering marvel, not a CAP violation.

> **Q: "How does eventual consistency resolve conflicts?"**  
> A: Common strategies:  
> (1) **Last-Write-Wins (LWW)** — highest timestamp wins (Cassandra uses this)  
> (2) **Vector Clocks** — track causal ordering, detect conflicts (DynamoDB)  
> (3) **CRDTs** — data structures that auto-merge without conflicts (used in collaborative editing)  
> (4) **Application-level resolution** — let the app decide (shopping cart: merge both versions)

> **Q: "In your design, where would you use strong vs eventual consistency?"**  
> A: **Within the same system, different components need different models:**  
> - Payment processing → **strong** (can't lose money)  
> - User profile updates → **read-your-own-writes** (you see your change, others catch up)  
> - Analytics dashboard → **eventual** (a few seconds delay is fine)  
> - Session store → **strong** (can't have user appear logged-in on one server and logged-out on another)

> **Q: "What is PACELC? How is it different from CAP?"**  
> A: PACELC extends CAP. It says:  
> - During a **P**artition: choose between **A**vailability and **C**onsistency (same as CAP)  
> - **E**lse (no partition): choose between **L**atency and **C**onsistency  
>  
> Example: DynamoDB is **PA/EL** — during partition it chooses availability, and during normal operation it chooses low latency (eventual consistency). A traditional RDBMS is **PC/EC** — always chooses consistency, even at the cost of latency.

